# Metal–Organic Frameworks as Multifunctional Solid Catalysts

Review
This review article describes the main reasons to use metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) as solid catalysts for liquid-phase reactions, including synthesis by design, high porosity in the micro-mesoporous range, and a high density of unsaturated transition-metal ions. The primary objective of this short review is to describe key current research strategies for improving activity, such as the role of structural defects, the synthesis of mixed-metal and mixed-ligand MOFs, and the synthesis of 2D materials with high lateral area-to-thickness aspect ratios. Emphasis is also placed on illustrating the potential offered by MOFs to develop multifunctional catalysts in tandem reactions. The final section contains our view on how the field will evolve towards implementing various industrial processes and expanding to electroand photocatalysis.
## Porous Solids in Catalysis
Heterogeneous catalysis occurs by interaction of active sites on a solid surface with substrates and reagents in the liquid or gas phase [1]. Since this interaction takes place at the interface between the solid and fluid phases, one of the most important parameters determining the catalytic activity of one material is the specific surface area where the active centers are located, as well as the accessibility to these sites [2]. For these reasons, most of the best-performing solid catalysts exhibit large specific surface area and increase in this parameter is a general strategy to increase the catalytic activity of a given material [3].
There are two main general strategies to increase the surface area of a material. One is based on the decrease in particle size down to the nanometric length scale [4]. A complementary alternative is the development of porous materials where the interior of the particle can be accessed through channels open to the external surface, allowing mass diffusion from the exterior to the interior of the particle when the kinetic diameter of the substrate is smaller than the pore diameter [5]. One example of a microporous material that has dominated catalysis in refining and petrochemistry is zeolites, which are crystalline aluminosilicates with open pores that permit the transit of molecules in and out of the crystallite [6,7].
While zeolites are top-performing catalysts for gas-phase reactions of linear hydrocarbons, several applications of zeolites in the liquid phase, particularly for fine chemicals, characterized by larger molecular dimensions, lower boiling points, and the simultaneous presence of different functional groups, have been hampered by the limited choice in pore size of conventional zeolites (e.g., faujasite ${\sim}0.74~\mathsf{n m},$ ) [8]. In this regard, there is a clear gap in the toolbox of heterogeneous catalysis for micro/mesoporous materials that could be used as solid catalysts in liquid-phase reactions [9]. Mesoporous aluminosilicates such as MCM-41 and SBA-15 do not completely fill this gap due to their mild acidity and lack of other types of active centers, together with a relatively low stability towards water/ moisture [10].
## Highlights
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are often used as solid catalysts due to their abundant unsaturated metal sites, high surface area, and tunable pore volume.
Possible strategies to enhance the catalytic activity of MOFs include generation of structural defects, introduction of electron withdrawing groups on the linker, and decreasing the dimensionality from 3D to 2D.
Encapsulation of metal complexes or nanoparticles inside MOFs favors higher catalytic activity through synergism between the guest and MOF.
MOFs can be designed as multifunctional catalysts having complementary types of sites to perform tandem processes comprising more than one elementary reaction.
## Metal–Organic Frameworks (MOFs) by Design
Most of the requirements, like high surface area, porosity, and high thermal/chemical stability needed for porous solids to act as heterogeneous catalysts in liquid-phase reactions, but also expanding to gas-phase reactions at moderate temperatures, are fulfilled by MOFs [11–14]. MOFs are crystalline porous coordination polymers having nodes of one or few metal ions that are coordinated with rigid bipodal/multipodal organic linkers, frequently aromatic polycarboxylates or nitrogenated heterocycles [15]. The directionality of the metal–ligand bond and rigidity of the organic ligands connecting more than one of these nodes determine how the lattice is built around the nodes creating open pore space. The empty volume of MOFs and their low framework density (i.e., mass in the unit cell volume) compare favorably with other types of porous solids. In addition, the pore size of some of MOFs is significantly larger than that of conventional large pore zeolites, ranging from micro-to-mesoporous [16–18].
A key reason underlying the importance of MOFs in heterogeneous catalysis is the possibility to design MOFs by appropriate selection of ligand size and geometry, as well as the nature of the metal nodes and their coordination geometry [19]. Knowing these parameters, it is generally possible to predict pore size, geometry, and stability of the material [15,20]. Similar predictive capacity has not yet been achieved for zeolites and is the basis of the ‘synthesis by design’ concept. This concept means that prediction (in advanced) of MOF structures and properties is possible, knowing the structure of the metal nodes, the coordination geometry around them, and the directionality of the coordination sites on the organic linker. It has been shown that isomorphic MOFs can be obtained by maintaining the metal nodes and using organic linkers with identical coordination directionality but varying molecular dimensions. Moreover, isomorphic crystal structures can be equally obtained for a series of metal ions with the same Coulombic charge and coordination geometry forming the same metal nodes if the organic ligand is maintained. Figure 1A illustrates the concept of synthesis by design.
## MOFs as Solid Lewis Acids
Due to the numerous nodal coordination positions, some are not involved in the construction of the porous lattice and are thus occupied by exchangeable solvent molecules or other monopodal ligands present during material synthesis. Consequently, the nature and composition of MOFs with high transition-metal content and possible exchangeable coordination positions around the transition metals, their high surface area, and porosity are advantageous for promoting Lewis acid catalysis and oxidation/reduction reactions. Readers are referred to existing reports for detailed knowledge on the use of MOFs as Lewis acid catalysts for organic reactions [21,22]. Figure 2A presents some Lewis acid-catalyzed reactions that have been frequently tested to evaluate the catalytic performances of MOFs.
## Role of Structural Defects on MOF Catalysis
Besides exchangeable positions around transition-metal cations that could be present in the ideal crystal lattice, increasing attention is being paid to the role of structural defects on the solid. These structural imperfections can generate coordinatively unsaturated positions around metal ions that otherwise should ideally be completely saturated. Typical defects are missing ligands or nodal metal ions, as shown in Figure 1B.
While defects can be formed inadvertently during the synthesis, a recent line of research is the purposeful generation of crystal defects [23]. One avenue during synthesis is the addition of a certain proportion of a different ligand with a structure closely related to the structural linker, but with one missing binding site [24]. For example, using the modulator benzoic acid to replace a certain proportion of terephthalic acid [25]. The presence of these modulators in appropriate proportion
