during synthesis may have a strong influence on the crystallinity of the resulting solid and, therefore, on the density of defects and catalysis. Figure 1C summarizes the purposeful generation of structural defects using modulators during the MOF synthesis.
Since the properties of MOFs are dominated by crystallinity that can be determined by X-ray diffraction (XRD), the density of defects is always difficult to assess and quantify. Analytical data, thermogravimetric analysis [26] and imaging studies [27] allow one to assess if there is a discrepancy between the theoretical formula for the ideal MOF structure and experimental values. This discrepancy is generally accepted as an indication of the existence of defects in the material.
## Influence of Linker Substituents on MOF Catalytic Activity
Besides metal nodes as active sites, MOFs as solid catalysts also offer the possibility of having ligands substituted with Brönsted acid (Figure 2B) or basic sites (Figure 2C) that can also participate in catalysis [28]. Besides the intrinsic activity of substituents on the organic linker as acid or basic groups, the presence of strong electron withdrawing or donor substituents on the ligands also offer a certain control on the acid strength of the nodal metals and their redox potential by tuning the electron density of the metal–linker coordination bond through inductive effects [29]. As it can be predicted by simple concepts from inductive effects on aromatic compounds, electron withdrawing groups (e.g., nitro groups) diminish the electron density of the metal cations of the nodes and, therefore, their presence should increase the catalytic activity of the MOF with a nitro substituted linker (compared with that of the parent MOF). This effect has been experimentally measured and a linear relationship of the initial reaction rate with the Hammett $\left(\upsigma_{\mathrm{meta}}\right)$ constant for reactions involving Lewis acid sites has been generally observed [29,30]. A linear relationship even holds for the variation of the redox potential for the series of substituted MOFs and with oxidation reactions involving molecular oxygen activation [31]. Thus, substitution of the aromatic linker in MOFs based on terephthalate with electron withdrawing groups appears to be a general methodology to increase further the catalytic activity of the parent MOF, very frequently over one order of magnitude [29,32].
## Structural Stability of MOFs
Although MOFs have many positive features for their use in heterogeneous catalysis, they have several weaknesses and pitfalls. A major drawback of many MOFs (but not all) is their lack of structural stability under reaction conditions and their inability to be reactivated by thermal treatment. In this way, there are some MOFs that change their structure upon storage due to the influence of moisture, such as MOF-5 [33]. Other MOFs cannot withstand contact with certain reagents, particularly those that can establish strong metal–ligand interactions or can cause the reduction of the metal ions [34]. As a general rule of thumb, MOFs comprised of divalent metal cations such as $Z n^{2+}$ or ${\mathsf{C U}}^{2+}$ are less stable than those containing tri- or tetra-positive metal ions that establish stronger Coulombic attraction with the negatively charged linkers [35].
Despite the inherent MOF instability derived from the strength of the metal–linker coordinative bonds and the organic nature of the linker, there are by now several examples of MOF structures that enjoy remarkable thermal and chemical stability. Examples of these robust MOFs are several MIL-based solids [MIL-100(Cr) and MIL-101 $(\mathsf{C r}^{3+}\mathsf{o r}\mathsf{F e}^{3+})]$ and several $Z\r^{r^{4+}}$ -based MOFs [36,37].
There are numerous examples providing compelling evidence that these MOFs can act as catalysts without undergoing collapse of the crystal structure or leaching of the metal ions.
## MOFs as Supports of Metal Nanoparticles (NPs)
Besides MOFs as catalysts with intrinsic activity due to the nature of the metal ions and linkers and the presence of free coordination positions, MOFs can also play a passive role as large surface area supports of other components responsible for the observed catalytic activity [38,39]. More specifically, structurally robust MOFs are among the preferred supports to incorporate noble metal NPs (see Figure 3A for three possible incorporation procedures). The resulting hybrid solids have been observed to be among the most efficient catalysts for reactions such as crosscoupling (Figure 3B) and oxidation reactions (Figure 4A). In these types of catalysts, MOFs acting as hosts should provide large pore volume to accommodate the active metal NPs and allow substrate accessibility to these NPs. However, they should stabilize the particle size via geometrical constraints or through ligand and/or nodal interactions. Particle size stabilization is among the major roles of a support, since the catalytic activity of metal NPs decreases or even disappears when the particle size goes much beyond $10\mathsf{n m}$ .
The interactions of hosted metal NPs and MOF constituents should generally meet the balance between growth stabilization and activity of the incorporated metal NPs because the number of linkers or nodes interacting with the guest NPs is low compared with the number of metal atoms that should still be free for interaction with substrates and reagents.
The presence of substituents on the organic linker can also play a role in increasing the stability of incorporated guests. In this sense, for instance, it has been shown that amino substitution at the terephthalate linkers of MIL-101 $(C\mathsf{r})$ increases the long-term stability of small Au NPs, about 3-nm in diameter, due to interaction between ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf N}^{\cdots}{\sf A u}$ of the linkers in the cage of MIL-101 $(C\mathsf{r})$ - $\cdot\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ [40]. Compared with the catalytic activity of the parent MIL-101(Cr), the catalytic activity for the aerobic alcohol oxidation of the $N H_{2}$ -substituted congener is coincident, but the stability of the catalyst upon reuse is higher when the $N H_{2}$ -groups are present. The limited number of $N H_{2}$ in the cages is apparently enough to stabilize the hosted Au NPs against particle growth, but they are insufficient to decrease the catalytic activity of encapsulated Au NPs for aerobic alcohol oxidation.
## Multifunctional Catalysis
Although heterogeneous catalysis by MOFs has become a mature field that has been intensively researched over the last 20 years, the field has been continuously evolving and new possibilities have been attracting considerable attention in the last few years. One of these new areas is the exploitation of the opportunities that MOFs offer as multifunctional catalysts for tandem reactions [41,42]. Tandem reactions in which two or more transformations are carried out in one-pot with a single catalyst correspond to a clear case of process intensification that decreases work-up steps, such as separation of intermediate products, while still resulting in selective and efficient processes.
Tandem reactions frequently require more than one type of active center. Generally different types of sites (‘orthogonal catalytic sites’) are more suited to develop tandem reactions, since the activity
