## Mixed-Metal/Mixed-Linker MOFs
Initially, most of the new MOFs contained a single metal at the nodes and a single organic linker. However, due to the dynamic nature of the metal–linker coordinative bond, it has been found that MOFs having two different transition metals with similar ionic radii at the nodes or two different linkers can be prepared. These mixed-metal or mixed-linker MOFs can be prepared either directly during the synthesis or by postsynthetic exchange of preformed MOFs either with different metal ions or linkers [45]. Like metal alloying, the presence of two metals in the linker can serve to modulate the Lewis acid strength. The softness/hardness balance of the Lewis acid sites, therefore, opens new avenues in heterogeneous catalysis [46].
As mentioned earlier, the use of more than one linker during synthesis can also be applied to gain control on the crystallinity of the MOFs and introduce framework defects. The presence of monocarboxylic acids (e.g., benzoic acid and acetic acid) during the synthesis of MOFs having dicarboxylic linkers has also been used to increase the crystallinity of the material. Alternatively, the synthesis can be performed in the presence of two aromatic linkers differing in the nature of the substituent on the aromatic ring and, in this way, mixed-linker MOFs can be prepared. Figure 6 shows one of the effectively utilized strategies for the preparation of mixed-linker MOFs by using a preformed dicarboxylic palladium complex in combination with 4,4′- biphenyldicarboxylate as linkers.
These mixed-linker MOFs are valuable in heterogeneous catalysis since they can readily offer anchoring sites at the linker to introduce additional active sites located at peripheral framework positions, as the solid resulting from the synthesis indicated in Figure 6. In these materials, there are simultaneously two metals, one with the function of building the MOF structure and the other as an immobilized metal complex, and two linkers, one bonded to two metals and the other bonded exclusively to the metal nodes.
There are two methods by which this type of mixed-linker MOF (having a metal complex as one of the linkers) can be prepared. In the first, appropriate selection of the optimal proportion of mixed linkers, one of which is already a metal complex having carboxylate binding units, is used as a building block in the MOF synthesis. In the second, mixed linkers are used to construct a MOF that subsequently is exposed to the metal ion that will be anchored in the satellite positions of the framework. In either case, these types of MOFs have been extensively used for performing large number of C–C bond-forming reactions, including Suzuki, Sonogashira, Heck, and Ullmann couplings [47–49].
## Two-Dimensional (2D) MOFs
Besides mixed-metal, mixed-linkers MOFs, or defect-containing samples in catalysis, one area that is gaining importance in the field of MOFs as heterogeneous catalysts is the activity of 2D MOFs [50]. In these 2D MOFs, the aspect ratio of lateral size and thickness of the particle is increased by decreasing the particle thickness (i.e., moving towards 2D materials). As an example, Figure 7 illustrates the case of a Hf-based 2D MOF. In these 2D MOFs, the accessibility of the active sites increases, becoming highly available for interaction with reagents without masstransfer limitations. In addition, 2D morphologies render these samples suitable for their
## Trends in Chemistry
incorporation in membranes and in the preparation of thin films. Moreover, although still not totally clarified, 2D MOFs appear to be more stable than analogous 3D samples, particularly for the case of dipositive cations such as $Z n^{2+}$ and $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ . Therefore, 2D materials can further increase the catalytic activity and stability of MOFs. Thus, the combination of site accessibility, high defect density, and stability is responsible for the increasing number of examples illustrating the superiority of 2D MOFs as heterogeneous catalysts in comparison with 3D MOFs [50].
The enhanced activity of 2D MOFs in catalysis was initially reported by Cao and coworkers. The activity of 2D MOF, namely Fe-TPY-MOL (TPY: $4^{\prime}$ -(4-benzoate)- $(2,2^{\prime},2^{\prime}$ ′terpyridine)- $.5,5^{\prime\prime}.$ - dicarboxylate; MOL: $H_{6}(\upmu_{3}-\mathsf{O})_{4}(\upmu_{3}-\mathsf{O}\mathsf{H})_{4}$ - $(\mathsf{H C O}_{2})_{6}(\mathsf{B T B})_{2}$ , (Figure 7) was studied for olefin hydrosilylation [51]. 2D Fe-TPY-MOL was able to promote almost complete styrene hydrosilylation providing selectively the anti-Markovnikov product $(\mathsf{T O N}{>}5000)$ , under conditions in which the analogous 3D Fe-TPY-MOF exhibited no activity. This contrasting activity between these two MOFs was attributed to the limited diffusion of reactants within the 3D MOF channels, while 2D MOFs have exposed active sites easily accessible to reactant molecules.
To put into perspective the importance of forming thin films, it has to be considered (for example) that these 2D materials open the use of MOFs as electrocatalysts, a field that has, until now, remained almost unexplored due to the electrically insulating properties of 3D MOF particles.
This limitation, however, is much less important when films of only few nanometers are used. Additionally, in photocatalytic applications, there are advantages of using 2D MOFs that can form films more readily [52] with respect to the use of analogous particulate 3D MOFs.
## Concluding Remarks
The present contribution shows that in only two decades MOFs have become among the preferred solid catalysts for liquid-phase reactions. The versatility of these materials with respect to their use as catalysts has been illustrated by highlighting several strategies to increase their catalytic activity, including the presence of substituents on the linker, the generation of defects, the use of mixed metals or linkers, the presence of hosted metal NPs, and 2D morphologies. Some of these approaches (like 2D MOFs) are very recent and many more reports on their activity and properties will be appearing soon because of ongoing research. Considering the design flexibility of MOFs, it can be forecasted that even further novel strategies will be reported soon. However, a key goal remains the development of industrial process-based MOF catalysts to produce fine chemicals (see Outstanding Questions).
Besides conventional thermal catalysis, MOFs are also widely used as photocatalysts, including ${\sf H}_{2}$ generation from water and $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction. Their use will continue, with emphasis on the oxidation semi reactions, particularly water oxidation, that have been considerably less explored. The performance of MOFs in this area has matched or even surpassed that of conventional metal oxide semiconductors. Further, approaches such as the use of 2D MOFs can have a large impact in this field. In this regard, it can be anticipated that the near future will see the development of 2D MOFs in electrocatalysis. It is expected that films of nanometric thickness can exhibit a high performance in electrocatalytic processes of relevance in the context of renewable energy, hopefully eliminating the need for noble and precious metals as electrodes. In any case, what appears evident is that the research in the field of MOFs as solid catalysts will continue at an increasing pace in the coming years.
